# /score-entities

Generalizes Topwater's `/score-companies`. Scores every discovered
{{vocabulary.entity}} against `originate.config.json`'s `criteria` block —
before a single dollar is spent on enrichment.

---

## Before anything

Load `standards/OUTPUT-STANDARDS.md` Rule O4 (reuse — unit is the
{{vocabulary.entity}}; a {{vocabulary.entity}} with no row in the prior
scored file is the only "new" work) and Rule O1 (checkpointing below
depends on this skill's own run folder existing before scoring starts).

## Checkpointing (this is the batch-level layer named in
`docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` §3 — implement it here, not elsewhere)

Score in small batches. As each batch finishes, save it to its own
checkpoint file immediately — never held until the whole run completes. If
a run is interrupted and picked back up, the next attempt reads whatever
checkpoint files already exist, treats those {{vocabulary.entityPlural}} as
done, and only re-does the batch that was actually still in progress. This
is what makes a large run (hundreds of {{vocabulary.entityPlural}}) safe to
interrupt without losing finished work.

---

## Scoring logic — compiled from `criteria`, not hand-written per tenant

1. **Immediate pass.** Check every rule in `criteria.immediatePass` first,
   before anything else. Any match means an immediate PASS for this
   {{vocabulary.entity}} — do not evaluate tiers, do not spend any further
   research on it.
2. **Tiers.** Evaluate `criteria.tiers` in list order. A
   {{vocabulary.entity}} matches the **first** tier whose `rules` all
   evaluate true against this {{vocabulary.entity}}'s known fields. A
   {{vocabulary.entity}} matching no tier is an outright pass, or the
   lowest-priority tier if the config defines one for that purpose.
3. **Judgment signals.** For `criteria.judgmentSignals`, apply holistic
   judgment — these are not hard gates, they inform which
   {{vocabulary.entityPlural}} within a tier are worth prioritizing first
   inside that tier, and they get stated explicitly in the output's
   rationale field, not silently folded into a score.
4. **Red flags.** For any `criteria.redFlags` signal present, do not
   auto-pass the {{vocabulary.entity}} — flag it explicitly and note the
   configured `escalateTo` target in the output. A red flag changes what
   gets surfaced to a human; it never silently changes the tier.
5. **Missing data.** Per `criteria.onMissingData` (default
   `null-and-flag`): never guess a missing field's value and never screen a
   {{vocabulary.entity}} out solely because a field is missing — set it
   null, flag it, and continue evaluating everything that can still be
   evaluated.

Steps 1-2 are deterministic and must be computed the same way every time
given the same input data — the actual comparison logic (rule field/op/
value evaluation) is the one piece of this skill that is genuinely code,
not prose judgment, and it is generated from the exact same `criteria`
JSON a human reviewed during onboarding (see
`docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` §4) — there is no second, hand-maintained copy of
these thresholds anywhere else to fall out of sync with.

---

## Output format

```json
{
  "{{pipeline.stageA.unitNoun|| "category"}}": "",
  "scored_at": "",
  "{{vocabulary.entityPlural}}": [
    {
      "name": "",
      "tier": "",
      "immediate_pass_matched": null,
      "matched_rules": [],
      "judgment_notes": "",
      "red_flags": [],
      "missing_fields": []
    }
  ]
}
```

Save per `standards/OUTPUT-STANDARDS.md` Rule O1. Produce a readable prose
summary (counts per tier, notable red flags) and render it per Rule O2 —
the JSON alone is never the human-facing artifact.
